# The Myth of the Twin Rivers

In the beginning, two rivers flowed from the same mountain spring—the River of Giving and the River of Keeping. Each was ruled by a spirit: Dara the Generous and Kael the Cautious.

One terrible drought threatened all the valley below. Dara's river, though depleted, still held water. Kael's river had nearly run dry.

Dara faced a choice: send water downstream to Kael, trusting it would multiply through underground springs and return stronger, or keep it safe within her banks.

"If I send my water through our ancient channels," Dara said, "the deep springs will triple it, and Kael can send some back. We'll both survive."

"But what if Kael keeps it all?" whispered her fearful tributaries.

Dara chose trust. She released her water into the connecting channels. The springs did multiply it threefold, and Kael received abundance.

Now Kael faced his choice: return water to honor their bond, or keep everything for himself.

Kael remembered droughts pass but neighbors remain. He returned half the multiplied waters.

Both rivers survived. And ever since, the valley people remember: what you send forward with trust returns as a flood—but only if both spirits choose reciprocity over fear.
